courses.learning_outcomes:
After this course, a student should be able to:

1. Find an appropriate research angle and research topic;
2. Perform literature search and literature review;
3. Conduct theoretical and empirical analysis;
4. Present research in the form of a research article.

This course is designed to enable students to go on to study applied field courses and to embark on
advanced studies in microeconomics in the future. Upon completing this course, the students are
expected to:

* Acquire a solid understanding of the core topics in microeconomics at a graduate level;
* Appreciate the structure of the theory core, building from individual behaviors to aggregate market activities;
* Be well equipped to study specialized theories in applied fields;
* Appreciate the use of modeling in furnishing theoretical arguments.

After completing this course, the students are expected to:

* Acquire advanced knowledge in core topics in microeconomics at the graduate level;
* Develop skills in using the fundamental microeconomic analytical tools to explain and predict market phenomena;
* Be well equipped to study specialized theories in applied fields.

After completing this course, the students are expected to:
1. Acquire basic knowledge in applied game theory;
2. Obtain preparations for conducting independent research.

This course aims to introduce mathematical methods that are essential for graduate study in economics. Upon finishing this course, students are expected to

1. master the basic mathematical methods to perform static and dynamic economic analysis;

2. appreciate the interconnection of mathematical methods and economic analysis;

3. develop skills to apply mathematical methods in economic analysis.

This course aims to introduce mathematical methods that are essential for graduate study in economics. Upon completing this course, students are expected to:

* Master the basic mathematical methods to perform static and dynamic economic analysis;
* Appreciate the interconnections between mathematical methods and economic analysis;
* Develop skills to apply mathematical tools in economic analysis.

This course treats economic theory mathematically and illustrates the mathematical methods with applications. Upon completing the course, students are expected to:

* Master the basic methods of static and dynamic optimizations;
* Apply the mathematical methods to economic models and applications;
* Develop basic analytical skills in performing economic analysis.

Upon successful completion of this course, students are expected to:

* Acquire advanced knowledge in asset pricing theory in Finance;
* Develop skills in financial modeling and analysis;
* Be able to apply analytical tools introduced in the course to study complex issues in financial markets.

After completing this course, the students are expected to:

1. Acquire advanced knowledge in theoretical and empirical analysis of international trade;

2. Develop skills in theoretic modeling and empirical analysis of trade;

3. Obtain preparations for conducting independent research in trade.

Upon completing this course, the student should:

* Develop ideas on the key topics of international finance, such as exchange rate determination and regimes;
* Be able to analyze policy-related real issues of international finance using the analytical tools covered.

Upon completing this course, students are expected to be able to:

* Outline the main theories and concepts in development economics;
* Summarize and evaluate empirical work in development economics;
* Make judgements about what policies the government should (or should not) pursue to foster economic development.

Upon completion of this course, students should be able to:

* Understand major public policies in China;
* Apply economic concepts and theories to analyze real-world public policy issues.
